## What this project is

__DESCRIPTION__

An Electron + Vite + React 19 app on the standard electron-vite single-app
layout (`src/main`, `src/preload`, `src/renderer`). All logic lives in
`src/main/core/`, a UI-free boundary enforced by tests, and the Electron
shell around it renders what core reports.

## Hard rules

**ESM import-path rule.** The app is `"type": "module"` with
`module: NodeNext`. Every intra-project import MUST carry a `.js` extension
even when the source file is `.ts` / `.tsx` — e.g.
`import { runExampleJob } from "./jobs/exampleJob.js"`. `tsc` does not rewrite
extensionless imports and Node fails at runtime with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`.

**Core isolation rule.** Nothing under `src/main/core/` may import `electron`,
`react`, `react-dom`, or any file outside `src/main/core/`. Core pushes
`JobEvent`s through `src/main/core/bus.ts`, and that bus is the only channel
into a UI. The rest of the main process imports core only via
`./core/index.js`, never a deeper path. The preload imports core types with
`import type` ONLY. `tests/core-boundary.test.ts` enforces all three;
`tests/core/export-surface.test.ts` guards the public surface.

**Native module rebuild.** `better-sqlite3` ships a Node-ABI binary, and the
app and the tests run on different runtimes. Two scripts:

- `npm run rebuild` — downloads the Electron prebuilt. Run before `npm run dev`
  and before packaging. `postinstall` runs it automatically.
- `npm run rebuild:node` — rebuilds from source against the current Node ABI.
  Run before `npm test`, which executes in plain Node and will otherwise fail
  with a `NODE_MODULE_VERSION` mismatch.

**State path is configured, not hardcoded.** Core resolves its state dir from
`__ENV_PREFIX___HOME`, then `configureAppHome`, then `~/.__MODULE_NAME__`. The
main process calls `configureAppHome(app.getPath("userData"))` at startup.
Tests point `__ENV_PREFIX___TEST_DB` (SQLite path) and `__ENV_PREFIX___HOME`
at a per-test tmp dir and clean up — never touch the real state dir.

## Commands

```bash
npm run dev                                # electron-vite dev, hot reload
npm test                                   # vitest
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run dist:mac                           # or dist:win / dist:linux
npx vitest run tests/core/exampleJob.test.ts   # single file
npx vitest run -t "returns aborted"            # single test by name
```

Run `npm run rebuild:node` after `npm install` and before the first
`npm test`; run `npm run rebuild` again before launching the app.

## Editing tips

- New event types go in `src/main/core/events.ts` and must be re-exported from
  `src/main/core/index.ts`, or the preload bridge cannot see them.
- New IPC channels need an `ipcMain.handle` in `src/main/ipc.ts` AND a method
  in `src/preload/api.ts` + `index.ts`. `tests/ipc-contract.test.ts` fails if
  the two drift.
- Adding a column to `runs` means editing `src/main/core/store/schema.ts` and
  `src/main/core/store/types.ts` together — `recordRun` and `listRuns` read
  the typed shape.
- `src/preload/api.ts` may only use `import type` from core. A value import
  pulls better-sqlite3 into the preload bundle and fails at load.
- IPC handlers stay thin: translate the payload, call core, translate back.
  Logic that lives in a handler cannot be tested without Electron.
- Long-running work takes an `AbortSignal` and returns a status instead of
  throwing on cancel, so the UI can always explain what happened.

## Style

Prefer functional modules over classes for job logic — plain functions with
explicit dependencies passed in. Keep the IPC surface small; add a channel only
when the UI actually needs it.
